select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 1, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.0", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow much of Northwind's copper purchase should be hedged?\nNorthwind Components, a manufacturer, must purchase 500,000 pounds of copper six months from today. Copper trades today at $4.00 per pound. A bank offers a contract that fixes the price of copper for delivery in six months at $4.10 per pound; the contract costs nothing to enter. The price that will prevail in six months is unknown. Select the fraction of the purchase to cover with the contract, then examine the cost of the purchase across the range of possible copper prices.\nTwo features of the figure deserve attention before any terminology is introduced. First, the contract does not make copper cheaper in every outcome; its effect is to narrow the range of possible costs. Second, the protection is symmetric: the position that removes the loss from a price increase also removes the gain from a price decrease. The contract offered by the bank is a forward contract, the simplest derivative, and the decision just made is a hedging decision. Both are defined precisely below."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.1#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.1", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.1", "heading": "What a derivative is", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.1 What a derivative is", "body": "A derivative is a financial contract whose value depends on the value of one or more other variables, called the underlying. The underlying is most often the price of a traded asset — a share of stock, a bond, a currency, a commodity such as copper or crude oil — but it may be any variable that can be observed and agreed upon: an interest rate, a stock index, the occurrence of a corporate default, or the temperature at a specified weather station. The contract is called a derivative because its value is derived from the underlying; it has no source of value independent of it.\nThe contract offered to Northwind illustrates the definition. Its underlying is the price of copper. If copper trades at $4.60 in six months, a contract to buy at $4.10 is valuable; if copper trades at $3.60, the same contract is a liability. Nothing about the contract changes between the two cases except the value of the underlying."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.1", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.1", "heading": "Key terms", "kind": "term", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.1 Key terms", "body": "Key terms: Derivative: a contract whose value depends on the value of an underlying variable. Underlying: the asset, rate, index, or event on which the contract is written. Maturity (or expiration date): the date on which the contract is settled or ceases to exist. Notional amount: the quantity of the underlying to which the contract refers, used to compute the payments; for Northwind, 500,000 pounds of copper."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.1", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.1", "heading": "What a derivative is", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.1 What a derivative is", "body": "Every derivative specifies, at a minimum, the underlying, the notional amount, the maturity, and a rule that converts the value of the underlying into a payment. This rule is the payoff function. Much of this book concerns two questions about a given payoff function: what the contract is worth today, and how the risk of holding it can be managed. This chapter introduces the principal contracts and the reasoning that later chapters develop into quantitative methods.\nFour contract types account for nearly all derivatives activity. A forward contract and a futures contract each commit two parties to a future transaction at a price fixed today. An option gives one party the right, without the obligation, to transact at a price fixed today. A swap commits two parties to exchange a series of cash flows. Sections 1.3 through 1.6 define each in turn."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.2#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.2", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.2", "heading": "Where derivatives trade", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.2 Where derivatives trade", "body": "Derivatives trade in two kinds of markets, which differ in how contracts are designed and in who bears the risk that a party fails to perform."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.2", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.2", "heading": "Exchange-traded markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.2 Exchange-traded markets", "body": "A derivatives exchange is an organized market in which the contracts are standardized by the exchange. The exchange specifies the underlying, the contract size, the delivery dates, and the settlement procedure; the participants negotiate only the price. Standardization concentrates trading in a small number of contracts and so makes them liquid, meaning that they can be bought or sold quickly at a price close to the last traded price.\nEach exchange operates or appoints a clearinghouse, an institution that becomes the legal counterparty to every trade: the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer. A trader on an exchange is therefore exposed to the clearinghouse and not to the unknown party on the other side of the trade. The clearinghouse protects itself by requiring each participant to deposit collateral, called margin, and by settling gains and losses every day. Chapter 2 describes this mechanism in detail."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.2", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.2", "heading": "Over-the-counter markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.2 Over-the-counter markets", "body": "In the over-the-counter (OTC) market, two parties negotiate a contract directly, usually with a bank acting as dealer on one side. The terms can be tailored to the needs of the client: Northwind's contract can be written for exactly 500,000 pounds and exactly the date on which the copper is required. The cost of this flexibility is counterparty credit risk, the risk that the other party fails to make a required payment. Since the financial crisis of 2007–2009, regulators in most jurisdictions have required standardized OTC contracts to be cleared through a central counterparty (CCP), which performs for the OTC market the function that a clearinghouse performs for an exchange.\nTable 1.1 Exchange-traded and over-the-counter markets compared\nFeatureExchange-tradedOver-the-counter\nContract termsStandardized by the exchangeNegotiated by the two parties\nCounterpartyThe clearinghouseThe other party, or a CCP if the contract is cleared\nCredit riskSmall; controlled by margin and daily settlementPresent; controlled by collateral agreements or central clearing\nLiquidityHigh in the actively traded contractsVaries with the contract; customized terms are costly to unwind"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.2", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.2", "heading": "Over-the-counter markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.2 Over-the-counter markets", "body": "Typical instrumentsFutures; listed optionsForwards; swaps; customized options"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.2", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.2", "heading": "The size of the market", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.2 The size of the market", "body": "The Bank for International Settlements (BIS) reports that the notional amount of OTC derivatives outstanding worldwide was $846 trillion at the end of June 2025. This figure is several times the value of world output, and it is frequently misread. The notional amount is the reference quantity from which payments are computed; for most contracts it is never exchanged. A more informative measure is the gross market value, the cost of replacing all outstanding contracts at current market prices, which the BIS reports as $21.8 trillion at the same date, or approximately 2.6 percent of the notional amount. Interest rate contracts form the largest category by a wide margin, followed by foreign exchange contracts.\nConcept check 1.1\nA corporation requires a contract on 37,500 barrels of jet fuel for delivery on a date that matches no listed contract. Which statement is correct?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.3#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.3", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.3", "heading": "Forward contracts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.3 Forward contracts", "body": "A forward contract is an agreement between two parties to buy or sell an asset at a specified future date for a price agreed today. The agreed price is the delivery price, denoted \\(K\\). No payment is made when the contract is entered; the delivery price is set so that the contract has zero value to both parties at inception. Forward contracts trade over the counter.\nThe party that agrees to buy the asset holds the long position; the party that agrees to sell holds the short position. These two terms recur throughout the book with the same meaning: a long position in any contract is the position of the buyer and gains when the price of the underlying rises; a short position is the position of the seller. Northwind, which agrees to buy copper, is long the forward; the bank is short.\nBy contrast, the spot price of an asset is its price for immediate delivery. Let \\(S_T\\) denote the spot price at the maturity date \\(T\\). The holder of the long position is obliged to pay \\(K\\) for an asset then worth \\(S_T\\). The payoff of a contract is the amount it delivers to its holder at maturity:\n\\[ \\text{Payoff to long forward} = S_T - K, \\qquad \\text{Payoff to short forward} = K - S_T . \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.3", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.3", "heading": "Forward contracts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.3 Forward contracts", "body": "Both payoffs are stated per unit of the underlying. Two properties follow at once. The payoff may be positive or negative, since both parties are obliged to transact. And the two payoffs sum to zero for every value of \\(S_T\\): the gain of one party is the loss of the other. A contract with this property is a zero-sum contract."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.3", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.3", "heading": "Forward contracts", "kind": "example", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.3 Forward contracts", "body": "Worked example 1.1 — Northwind's forward purchase\nNorthwind enters a long forward on 500,000 pounds of copper at \\(K = \\$4.10\\). Find the payoff of the forward and the net cost of the copper if the spot price at maturity is (a) $4.60 and (b) $3.60.\n1\nCase (a). Payoff per pound is \\(S_T - K = 4.60 - 4.10 = 0.50\\). On 500,000 pounds the forward pays \\(0.50 \\times 500{,}000 = \\$250{,}000\\).\n2\nNorthwind buys copper in the spot market for \\(4.60 \\times 500{,}000 = \\$2{,}300{,}000\\). Net cost: \\(2{,}300{,}000 - 250{,}000 = \\$2{,}050{,}000\\).\n3\nCase (b). Payoff per pound is \\(3.60 - 4.10 = -0.50\\); the forward loses $250,000. Spot cost is \\(3.60 \\times 500{,}000 = \\$1{,}800{,}000\\). Net cost: \\(1{,}800{,}000 + 250{,}000 = \\$2{,}050{,}000\\).\n4\nConclusion. In both cases the net cost is \\(4.10 \\times 500{,}000 = \\$2{,}050{,}000\\). The forward fixes the cost at the delivery price whatever the spot price proves to be. In case (b) Northwind would have paid less without the contract; the forward removes favorable outcomes together with unfavorable ones."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.3", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.3", "heading": "Forward contracts", "kind": "warning", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.3 Forward contracts", "body": "Common error\nA forward that ends with a negative payoff was not, for that reason, a poor decision. The purpose of Northwind's hedge is a known cost, and the hedge delivers a cost of $2,050,000 in every case. A hedge should be evaluated by the risk it removed and not by a comparison, available only after the fact, with the outcome of not hedging."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.4#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.4", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.4", "heading": "Futures contracts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.4 Futures contracts", "body": "A futures contract, like a forward, is an agreement to buy or sell an asset at a future date for a price agreed today. It differs from a forward in three respects. It trades on an exchange. Its terms are standardized: a copper futures contract on the COMEX division of the CME Group, for example, covers 25,000 pounds of copper of a specified grade for delivery in a specified month. And its gains and losses are settled every trading day through the margin account of each party, a procedure known as daily settlement or marking to market, in place of a single settlement at maturity.\nThe price agreed in a futures contract is the futures price. At maturity the futures price converges to the spot price, and the total gain to a long position over the life of the contract equals the change in the futures price, as for a forward. Northwind could hedge with futures by taking a long position in 20 contracts, since \\(20 \\times 25{,}000 = 500{,}000\\) pounds. Most futures positions are closed before maturity by entering the opposite trade, and the hedger then buys the physical commodity in the spot market in the usual way."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.4#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.4", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.4", "heading": "Futures contracts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.4 Futures contracts", "body": "The economic content of futures and forwards is therefore the same, and for the purposes of this chapter the payoff \\(S_T - K\\) describes both. The institutional differences — margin, daily settlement, and the consequences of standardization for the quality of a hedge — are the subject of Chapters 2 and 5."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Options", "body": "A forward obliges both parties to transact. An option obliges only one of them. A call option gives its holder the right, but not the obligation, to buy the underlying asset at a specified price on or before a specified date. A put option gives its holder the right, but not the obligation, to sell the underlying asset at a specified price on or before a specified date. The specified price is the strike price or exercise price, denoted \\(K\\); the date is the expiration date. Using the right is called exercising the option.\nA European option can be exercised only on the expiration date. An American option can be exercised at any time up to and including that date. The names describe the exercise rule and carry no geographic meaning; both kinds trade on every continent. Unless stated otherwise, the options in this chapter are European."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Options", "body": "The party that buys an option holds the long position. The party that sells it holds the short position and is said to have written the option; this party is the writer. Because a right without an obligation cannot have negative value to its holder, the buyer must pay the writer for it. The price paid is the option premium. This is the essential difference from a forward: a forward costs nothing to enter and may end with a loss; an option costs the premium to acquire and can lose no more than that premium."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Payoffs at expiration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Payoffs at expiration", "body": "The holder of a call will exercise at expiration only if the asset is worth more than the strike price. If \\(S_T > K\\), exercising yields \\(S_T - K\\); otherwise the option expires unexercised and yields nothing. The holder of a put will exercise only if \\(S_T < K\\). The payoffs per unit of the underlying are therefore\n\\[ \\text{Long call: } \\max(S_T - K,\\, 0), \\qquad \\text{Long put: } \\max(K - S_T,\\, 0). \\]\nThe writer of each option receives the negative of these amounts: \\(-\\max(S_T - K, 0)\\) for a short call and \\(-\\max(K - S_T, 0)\\) for a short put. The profit of a position is its payoff adjusted for the premium: the buyer's profit is the payoff less the premium paid, and the writer's profit is the premium received less the amount paid out. For clarity of exposition this chapter ignores the interest that could be earned on the premium between purchase and expiration; later chapters account for it.\nThe stock price at which the profit of an option position is exactly zero is the breakeven price. For a call with premium \\(c\\) it is \\(K + c\\); for a put with premium \\(p\\) it is \\(K - p\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Payoffs at expiration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Payoffs at expiration", "body": "Table 1.2 The four basic option positions, per unit of the underlying (\\(c\\), \\(p\\) = call and put premiums)\nPositionPayoff at expirationProfitMaximum gainMaximum loss\nLong call\\(\\max(S_T-K,0)\\)\\(\\max(S_T-K,0)-c\\)Unlimited\\(c\\)\nShort call\\(-\\max(S_T-K,0)\\)\\(c-\\max(S_T-K,0)\\)\\(c\\)Unlimited\nLong put\\(\\max(K-S_T,0)\\)\\(\\max(K-S_T,0)-p\\)\\(K-p\\)\\(p\\)\nShort put\\(-\\max(K-S_T,0)\\)\\(p-\\max(K-S_T,0)\\)\\(p\\)\\(K-p\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Payoffs at expiration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Payoffs at expiration", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 1.1 — Payoff and profit at expiration\nSix basic positions\nSelect a position and adjust the strike or delivery price, the premium, and the price of the underlying at expiration. The solid line is the profit; the dashed line is the payoff before the premium. Verify each entry of Table 1.2 against the figure."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Payoffs at expiration", "kind": "example", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Payoffs at expiration", "body": "Worked example 1.2 — A call and a put on Northwind shares\nNorthwind shares trade at $28. A three-month European call with strike $30 costs $1.50 per share, and a three-month European put with strike $26 costs $1.20 per share. Each listed contract covers 100 shares. Find the profit on one contract of each if the share price at expiration is (a) $35 and (b) $22, and find each breakeven price.\n1\nCall, \\(S_T = 35\\). Payoff \\(=\\max(35-30,0)=5.00\\). Profit per share \\(=5.00-1.50=3.50\\); per contract, \\(3.50\\times100=\\$350\\).\n2\nCall, \\(S_T = 22\\). Payoff \\(=\\max(22-30,0)=0\\). The option expires unexercised; the loss is the premium, \\(1.50\\times100=\\$150\\).\n3\nPut, \\(S_T = 35\\). Payoff \\(=\\max(26-35,0)=0\\). Loss \\(=1.20\\times100=\\$120\\).\n4\nPut, \\(S_T = 22\\). Payoff \\(=\\max(26-22,0)=4.00\\). Profit per share \\(=4.00-1.20=2.80\\); per contract, $280.\n5\nBreakeven prices. Call: \\(K+c=30+1.50=\\$31.50\\). Put: \\(K-p=26-1.20=\\$24.80\\). The call buyer profits only if the share price rises by more than 12.5 percent from $28; a rise to $31 leaves the option exercised but the position at a loss of $0.50 per share."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Key terms — moneyness", "kind": "term", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Key terms — moneyness", "body": "Key terms — moneyness: An option is in the money if immediate exercise would produce a positive payoff (\\(S > K\\) for a call, \\(S < K\\) for a put), at the money if \\(S = K\\), and out of the money otherwise. Both options in Worked example 1.2 are out of the money when purchased."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.5#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.5", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.5", "heading": "Payoffs at expiration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.5 Payoffs at expiration", "body": "Concept check 1.2\nAn investor writes one put contract (100 shares) with strike $40 for a premium of $3 per share. At expiration the share price is $34. What is the investor's profit?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.6#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.6", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.6", "heading": "Swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.6 Swaps", "body": "A swap is an agreement between two parties to exchange cash flows on a series of future dates according to a specified rule. In the most common form, the plain vanilla interest rate swap, one party pays interest at a fixed rate on a notional principal and receives interest at a floating rate, one that is reset each period to a market reference rate, on the same principal. Only the difference between the two interest amounts changes hands on each date, and the principal itself is never exchanged.\nA swap can be understood as a portfolio of forward contracts, one for each payment date, and this observation is the basis of its valuation. A company with floating-rate debt that wishes to fix its interest cost can enter a swap to pay fixed and receive floating; the floating receipts offset the floating payments on the debt and leave a fixed net cost. Interest rate swaps are the largest single category of derivatives by notional amount. Chapter 7 treats swaps in full."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.7#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.7", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.7", "heading": "Why derivatives are traded: three kinds of participant", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.7 Why derivatives are traded: three kinds of participant", "body": "Participants in derivatives markets are conventionally divided into three groups according to their purpose. Hedgers use derivatives to reduce a risk that they already bear. Speculators use derivatives to take on risk in pursuit of profit, on the basis of a view about the future direction of a market variable. Arbitrageurs take offsetting positions in two or more instruments to lock in a profit without bearing risk. The same contract serves all three; what differs is the position the participant holds before the trade."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.7#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.7", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.7", "heading": "Hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.7 Hedging", "body": "Northwind's forward purchase of copper is a hedge: the company is exposed to an increase in the price of copper, and the long forward gains when that price rises. The general rule is that a hedge is a derivatives position whose gains occur in the circumstances in which the existing exposure produces losses. A firm that will buy an asset hedges with a long forward; a firm that will sell an asset hedges with a short forward.\nOptions provide a different kind of hedge. Consider an investor who owns 1,000 Northwind shares, worth $28,000, and fears a decline over the next three months. The investor may buy ten put contracts with strike $26 at $1.20 per share, for a total premium of $1,200. If the share price falls below $26, the puts are exercised and the shares are sold for $26,000; net of the premium the investor realizes no less than $24,800. If the share price rises, the puts expire unexercised and the investor retains the entire gain on the shares, less the $1,200 premium."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.7#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.7", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.7", "heading": "Hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.7 Hedging", "body": "The comparison is fundamental. A forward hedge fixes the outcome and costs nothing at inception, but gives up the benefit of favorable price movements. An option hedge sets a limit on the adverse outcome while preserving the favorable one, and for this asymmetry the hedger pays a premium. An option used in this way is a form of insurance, and the premium is the price of that insurance."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.7#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.7", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.7", "heading": "Speculation and leverage", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.7 Speculation and leverage", "body": "A speculator who expects Northwind shares to rise can buy the shares or buy call options. The two choices differ in leverage: the extent to which a given percentage change in the price of the underlying produces a larger percentage change in the value of the position."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.7#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.7", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.7", "heading": "Speculation and leverage", "kind": "example", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.7 Speculation and leverage", "body": "Worked example 1.3 — Leverage in an option position\nA speculator has $4,200 and expects Northwind, at $28, to rise within three months. Compare (i) buying shares with (ii) buying the $30-strike call at $1.50, if the share price at expiration is $35 and if it is $29.\n1\nPositions. (i) \\(4{,}200/28=150\\) shares. (ii) \\(4{,}200/1.50=2{,}800\\) calls, that is, 28 contracts.\n2\n\\(S_T=35\\). Shares: \\(150\\times(35-28)=\\$1{,}050\\), a return of 25 percent. Calls: payoff \\(2{,}800\\times5=14{,}000\\); profit \\(14{,}000-4{,}200=\\$9{,}800\\), a return of 233 percent.\n3\n\\(S_T=29\\). Shares: \\(150\\times1=\\$150\\), a return of 3.6 percent. Calls: the payoff is zero because \\(29<30\\); the loss is $4,200, a return of −100 percent.\n4\nConclusion. The option position magnifies both outcomes. The speculator was correct about the direction of the price in both cases, yet in the second case lost the entire investment because the rise was too small. A view on direction is not sufficient for an option position to profit; the magnitude and the timing of the movement matter as well."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.7#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.7", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.7", "heading": "Arbitrage", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.7 Arbitrage", "body": "An arbitrage opportunity is a trading strategy that requires no net investment, has no possibility of a loss, and has a positive probability of a gain. The simplest case involves the same asset trading in two markets. Suppose that a share trades at $28.00 in New York and at £22.00 in London when the exchange rate is $1.30 per pound. The London price is equivalent to \\(22.00\\times1.30=\\$28.60\\). A trader who simultaneously buys in New York and sells in London earns $0.60 per share, before transaction costs, without bearing any risk.\nSuch an opportunity cannot persist. Purchases in New York raise the price there, sales in London lower the price there, and the two prices converge until the remaining difference is no larger than the cost of trading. The proposition that two assets, or two portfolios, with identical future payoffs must have the same price today is the law of one price.\nPrinciple 1.1 — No arbitrage"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.7#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.7", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.7", "heading": "Arbitrage", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.7 Arbitrage", "body": "In a market in which participants can trade freely, prices adjust until no arbitrage opportunity remains. Consequently, if a portfolio of traded assets reproduces the payoff of a derivative in every future state, the value of the derivative today must equal the cost of that portfolio.\nA portfolio that reproduces the payoff of a derivative is a replicating portfolio. Principle 1.1 is the foundation of the theory of derivatives valuation. It requires no forecast of the price of the underlying and no assumption about the attitudes of investors toward risk; it requires only that identical payoffs cannot sell at different prices. Every valuation formula in this book, from the forward price in Chapter 4 to the Black–Scholes–Merton formula in Chapter 12, is an application of this single principle. Section 1.9 presents the first such application.\nConcept check 1.3\nA grain exporter will receive 2 million euros in four months and will convert them to dollars. The exporter enters a forward contract today to sell 2 million euros in four months at $1.10 per euro. How should this trade be classified?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.8#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.8", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.8", "heading": "The value of a derivative before maturity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.8 The value of a derivative before maturity", "body": "Payoff diagrams describe a contract at a single date, its maturity. Before maturity, the value of a derivative depends on the current price of the underlying and also on the time that remains. An option that is out of the money today is not worthless, because the price of the underlying may yet move through the strike. The value of an option can accordingly be separated into two parts. Its intrinsic value is the payoff it would have if it were exercised immediately, \\(\\max(S-K,0)\\) for a call. Its time value is the excess of its market value over its intrinsic value."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.8#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.8", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.8", "heading": "The value of a derivative before maturity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.8 The value of a derivative before maturity", "body": "Interactive figure 1.2 presents the value of the $30-strike call on Northwind shares as a surface over two variables: the share price and the time remaining to expiration. The surface is computed from the Black–Scholes–Merton model, derived in Chapter 12, with a risk-free interest rate of 5 percent and a volatility of 39 percent per year; at a share price of $28 with three months remaining, the model value is approximately $1.52, close to the premium used in the examples above. The model need not be understood at this stage. The purpose of the figure is geometric: as the time remaining falls to zero, the smooth surface descends onto the angular payoff diagram of Section 1.5."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.8#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.8", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.8", "heading": "The value of a derivative before maturity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.8 The value of a derivative before maturity", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 1.2 — Three-dimensional view\nValue of a European call as a function of share price and time to expiration\nDrag to rotate the surface; scroll or pinch to zoom. The gold curve is the value at the selected time to expiration; the dark line at the front edge is the payoff at expiration. Move the time slider toward zero and observe the gold curve settle onto the payoff.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.8#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.8", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.8", "heading": "The value of a derivative before maturity", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.8 The value of a derivative before maturity", "body": "Three observations from the figure anticipate later chapters. The value of the call is never below its intrinsic value. The time value is greatest when the share price is near the strike and declines as the option moves far into or out of the money. And for a fixed share price the value declines as expiration approaches, a property known as time decay, which Chapter 14 measures with the quantity called theta."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.9#36", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.9", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.9", "heading": "★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 36, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.9 ★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "body": "Optional section. Sections marked with a star present derivations that may be omitted on a first reading without loss of continuity. This section uses continuous compounding: an amount \\(A\\) invested at the continuously compounded annual rate \\(r\\) for \\(T\\) years grows to \\(Ae^{rT}\\). Chapter 3 develops compounding conventions in full.\nSection 1.3 stated that the delivery price of a forward is set so that the contract has zero value at inception. The delivery price with this property is the forward price, denoted \\(F_0\\). Principle 1.1 determines it exactly.\nProposition 1.1 — Forward price of an asset that pays no income\nLet \\(S_0\\) be the spot price of an asset that pays no dividends or other income before date \\(T\\) and that can be stored at no cost, and let \\(r\\) be the continuously compounded risk-free interest rate for maturity \\(T\\). If there are no arbitrage opportunities, the forward price for delivery at \\(T\\) is \\[ F_0 = S_0\\,e^{rT}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.9#37", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.9", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.9", "heading": "★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 37, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.9 ★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "body": "Proof. Consider two strategies, each of which delivers one unit of the asset at date \\(T\\). Under strategy A, the investor enters a long forward at delivery price \\(F_0\\) and pays \\(F_0\\) at \\(T\\). Under strategy B, the investor borrows \\(S_0\\) today, buys the asset, holds it, and repays \\(S_0e^{rT}\\) at \\(T\\). Neither strategy requires a net outlay today, and each leaves the investor holding one unit of the asset at \\(T\\). By the law of one price, the payments at \\(T\\) must be equal."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.9#38", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.9", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.9", "heading": "★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 38, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.9 ★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "body": "To confirm that any other price permits arbitrage, suppose first that \\(F_0 > S_0e^{rT}\\). An arbitrageur borrows \\(S_0\\), buys the asset, and enters a short forward. At \\(T\\) the asset is delivered for \\(F_0\\) and the loan is repaid, leaving \\(F_0 - S_0e^{rT} > 0\\) with certainty and no initial investment. This strategy is a cash-and-carry arbitrage. Suppose instead that \\(F_0 < S_0e^{rT}\\). An arbitrageur sells the asset short — that is, borrows the asset and sells it, with an obligation to return it later — invests the proceeds \\(S_0\\) at the rate \\(r\\), and enters a long forward. At \\(T\\) the investment is worth \\(S_0e^{rT}\\), the asset is bought for \\(F_0\\) under the forward and returned, and \\(S_0e^{rT} - F_0 > 0\\) remains. This is a reverse cash-and-carry arbitrage. Since both inequalities admit arbitrage, \\(F_0 = S_0e^{rT}\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.9#39", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.9", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.9", "heading": "★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "kind": "example", "ord": 39, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.9 ★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "body": "Worked example 1.4 — Detecting a mispriced forward\nNorthwind shares trade at $28 and will pay no dividend in the next six months. The six-month risk-free rate is 5 percent per year, continuously compounded. A dealer quotes a six-month forward price of $29.50. Is there an arbitrage opportunity?\n1\nNo-arbitrage forward price. \\(F_0 = 28\\,e^{0.05\\times0.5} = 28\\times1.025315 = \\$28.71\\).\n2\nComparison. The quoted price, $29.50, exceeds $28.71. The forward is overpriced; it should be sold, and the share bought.\n3\nStrategy today. Borrow $28 for six months; buy one share; enter a short forward at $29.50. Net cash flow today: zero.\n4\nIn six months. Deliver the share and receive $29.50; repay \\(28e^{0.025}=\\$28.71\\). Riskless profit: \\(29.50-28.71=\\$0.79\\) per share, whatever the share price is on that date."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.9#40", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.9", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.9", "heading": "★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 40, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.9 ★ A first no-arbitrage valuation: the forward price of a stock", "body": "The proposition contains no expectation of the future spot price. Two investors who disagree entirely about the prospects of Northwind must nonetheless agree on its forward price, because that price is fixed by the cost of the replicating strategy. Chapter 4 extends the result to assets that pay income, to currencies, and to commodities with storage costs."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.10#41", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.10", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.10", "heading": "The risks of derivatives", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 41, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.10 The risks of derivatives", "body": "The leverage that makes derivatives efficient instruments for hedging makes them equally efficient instruments for taking large risks, and a position that was established as a hedge can become a speculation if it is enlarged beyond the exposure it was meant to offset. Several of the largest trading losses on record arose in this manner or from positions that were not supervised adequately.\nTable 1.3 Selected derivatives losses\nInstitutionYearInstrumentsApproximate lossPrincipal cause\nMetallgesellschaft1993Oil futures$1.3 billionShort-dated futures hedging long-dated supply commitments; margin calls exhausted liquidity\nBarings Bank1995Nikkei 225 futures and options£827 millionUnauthorized speculation by a single trader who also controlled settlement\nLong-Term Capital Management1998Swaps and other relative-value positions$4.6 billionExtreme leverage; positions that lost value together in a crisis\nSociété Générale2008Equity index futures€4.9 billionUnauthorized positions concealed by fictitious offsetting trades\nAmerican International Group2008Credit default swapsGovernment support of $182 billionUnhedged sale of credit protection; collateral calls as credit markets fell"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.10#42", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.10", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.10", "heading": "The risks of derivatives", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 42, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.10 The risks of derivatives", "body": "The cases share common elements: positions large relative to capital, inadequate separation between those who trade and those who record and monitor the trades, and insufficient attention to the cash required to maintain a position before it matures. The final chapter of this book returns to these episodes with the analytical tools developed in the intervening chapters. The lesson to carry forward is that the purpose of a derivatives position must be defined before the position is taken, and its risk measured continuously thereafter."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.10#43", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.10", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.10", "heading": "The risks of derivatives", "kind": "notes", "ord": 43, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.10 The risks of derivatives", "body": "Historical note\nContracts for future delivery are far older than modern finance. Rice merchants at the Dōjima exchange in Osaka traded standardized contracts on future rice deliveries from the early eighteenth century, and the shogunate recognized the market officially in 1730. The Chicago Board of Trade, founded in 1848, standardized grain contracts in the following decades to serve the farmers and merchants of the American Midwest.\nTwo events in the spring of 1973 mark the beginning of the modern market. The Chicago Board Options Exchange opened for trading in listed call options on sixteen stocks in April, and in the same year Fischer Black and Myron Scholes, and separately Robert Merton, published the valuation theory that bears their names. For this work Scholes and Merton received the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in 1997; Black had died in 1995."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.summary#44", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.summary", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 44, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.summary Summary", "body": "A derivative is a contract whose value depends on an underlying variable. Its payoff function converts the value of the underlying at maturity into a payment.\nDerivatives trade on exchanges, where contracts are standardized and a clearinghouse stands between the parties, and over the counter, where contracts are negotiated and counterparty credit risk must be managed.\nA forward or futures contract obliges both parties to transact at a price fixed today. The long position pays \\(S_T-K\\) and the short position \\(K-S_T\\).\nAn option gives its holder a right without an obligation, in exchange for a premium. A call pays \\(\\max(S_T-K,0)\\) and a put \\(\\max(K-S_T,0)\\); profit equals payoff adjusted for the premium.\nHedgers reduce an existing risk, speculators accept risk on the basis of a view, and arbitrageurs lock in riskless profit from mispricing.\nThe absence of arbitrage implies that a derivative is worth the cost of the portfolio that replicates it. For an asset that pays no income, the forward price is \\(F_0=S_0e^{rT}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises#45", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 45, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.exercises Exercises", "body": "1.1 An investor enters a short forward contract to sell 100,000 British pounds at $1.2700 per pound. What is the investor's gain or loss if the exchange rate at maturity is (a) $1.2500 and (b) $1.3000?\nThe short forward pays \\(K-S_T\\) per pound. (a) \\((1.2700-1.2500)\\times100{,}000=\\$2{,}000\\) gain. (b) \\((1.2700-1.3000)\\times100{,}000=-\\$3{,}000\\), a loss of $3,000.\n1.2 A European call with strike $50 costs $2.50. Write the profit of the buyer as a function of \\(S_T\\), state the breakeven price, and state the range of \\(S_T\\) over which the option is exercised.\nProfit \\(=\\max(S_T-50,0)-2.50\\). Breakeven: \\(50+2.50=\\$52.50\\). The option is exercised whenever \\(S_T>50\\). For \\(50<S_T<52.50\\) it is exercised at a net loss, because exercise recovers part of the premium.\n1.3 An investor writes five put contracts (100 shares each) with strike $60 for a premium of $4 per share. Compute the profit if the share price at expiration is (a) $66, (b) $58, and (c) $50. What is the maximum possible loss?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises#46", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 46, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.exercises Exercises", "body": "Profit per share \\(=4-\\max(60-S_T,0)\\), on 500 shares. (a) \\(4\\times500=\\$2{,}000\\). (b) \\((4-2)\\times500=\\$1{,}000\\). (c) \\((4-10)\\times500=-\\$3{,}000\\). The maximum loss occurs at \\(S_T=0\\): \\((4-60)\\times500=-\\$28{,}000\\).\n1.4 A share trades at $40. A three-month call with strike $42 costs $2. A speculator has $8,000. Compare the dollar profit and the percentage return from buying shares and from buying calls if the share price in three months is (a) $48 and (b) $41.\nShares: 200. Calls: 4,000. (a) Shares: \\(200\\times8=\\$1{,}600\\) (20%). Calls: \\(4{,}000\\times6-8{,}000=\\$16{,}000\\) (200%). (b) Shares: \\(200\\times1=\\$200\\) (2.5%). Calls: payoff zero; loss $8,000 (−100%).\n1.5 A United States importer must pay 50 million yen in three months. Describe a hedge using (a) a forward contract and (b) an option, stating in each case the position taken, and explain the difference between the two outcomes."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises#47", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 47, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.exercises Exercises", "body": "(a) A long forward to buy 50 million yen fixes the dollar cost at the forward rate, whatever the spot rate. (b) A call option on yen (the right to buy yen at a strike exchange rate) sets a maximum dollar cost equal to the strike plus the premium, while leaving the importer free to buy at the spot rate if the yen depreciates. The forward costs nothing at inception and removes both adverse and favorable outcomes; the option requires a premium and removes only the adverse outcomes.\n1.6 Explain why the writer of an option, unlike the buyer, is normally required by an exchange to post margin.\nAfter paying the premium, the buyer has no further obligation and so cannot default. The writer has an obligation to pay \\(\\max(S_T-K,0)\\) or \\(\\max(K-S_T,0)\\) on exercise, an amount that may be many times the premium received. Margin secures the performance of that obligation to the clearinghouse.\n1.7 ★ A share that pays no dividend trades at $80. The one-year risk-free rate is 4 percent, continuously compounded. (a) Compute the one-year forward price. (b) A dealer quotes $82.00. Describe the arbitrage and compute the profit per share."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises#48", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 48, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.exercises Exercises", "body": "(a) \\(F_0=80e^{0.04}=80\\times1.040811=\\$83.26\\). (b) The quoted forward is below \\(F_0\\), so it is underpriced: enter a long forward, sell the share short for $80, and invest the proceeds. In one year the investment is worth $83.26; buy the share for $82.00 under the forward and return it. Profit: \\(83.26-82.00=\\$1.26\\) per share (reverse cash-and-carry).\n1.8 “The notional amount of derivatives outstanding exceeds world output several times over; the market is therefore a threat to the world economy of the same magnitude.” Evaluate this statement."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises#49", "source_key": "fd-textbook:1.exercises", "chapter": 1, "section": "1.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 49, "title": "Chapter 1, Derivative Markets and Instruments — 1.exercises Exercises", "body": "The inference is unsound. The notional amount is a reference quantity for computing payments and, for most contracts, is never exchanged; the amount at risk is far smaller. Gross market value, the replacement cost of outstanding contracts, was about 2.6 percent of notional at mid-2025, and netting and collateral reduce exposure further. The statement is correct only to the extent that notional amounts indicate the scale of activity and that concentrated, poorly collateralized exposures can transmit losses, as in 2008. The appropriate measures of risk are market value, exposure after netting and collateral, and the concentration of that exposure."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
